Day 22: PostgreSQL – DDL & DML Basics

Topic:  
Introduction to Data Definition Language (DDL) and Data Manipulation Language (DML) in PostgreSQL, focusing on creating tables, defining data types, and performing basic data manipulation operations.  

Learning Objectives:  
• Understand DDL commands such as `CREATE TABLE` and their role in database design.  
• Explore PostgreSQL data types like SERIAL, TEXT, and BOOLEAN.  
• Apply DML commands to insert and manipulate data in tables.  
• Compare PostgreSQL syntax with other database systems.  

Activities / Deliverables:  
• Recreate VolyoDB tables in PostgreSQL using DDL commands.  
• Insert sample data into the tables using DML commands.  
• Analyze and compare PostgreSQL syntax with VolyoDB syntax.  

Important Concepts:  
• DDL focuses on defining and altering database structures (e.g., tables, schemas).  
• DML focuses on manipulating data within tables (e.g., inserting, updating, deleting).  

SQL Syntax / Commands:  
• `CREATE TABLE table_name (column1 datatype, column2 datatype, ...);`  
• `INSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2, ...) VALUES (value1, value2, ...);`  

Real-world Use Cases:  
• Designing and managing database schemas for applications.  
• Migrating database tables from one system (e.g., VolyoDB) to PostgreSQL.  

Tools / Tech:  
• PostgreSQL  
• pgAdmin 4  

Summary:  
Learned the basics of DDL and DML in PostgreSQL, including creating tables and inserting data. Explored PostgreSQL-specific data types compared other databases and practiced migrating tables from VolyoDB to PostgreSQL.  

